      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2017 John Wiley & Sons A/S. Published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "PROBLEM",
            "content": "Women with antiphospholipid antibodies (aPLs) present a risk of pregnancy morbidity (PM), vascular thrombosis (VT), or both (PM/VT). aPLs affect trophoblast function, and the aim of this study was to determine the modulation of this aPL-induced damage by different drugs."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHOD OF STUDY",
            "content": "IgG was obtained from women with PM and PM/VT positive to aPLs. Binding of IgG to trophoblastic cells, proliferation, mitochondrial membrane integrity, and trophoblast invasion were assessed. The effect of enoxaparin, aspirin, and aspirin-triggered lipoxin (ATL) were evaluated as well as signal transducer and activator of transcription 3 (STAT3) phosphorylation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "IgG from women with aPLs strongly binds to trophoblastic cells. Integrity of mitochondrial membrane was reduced, and proliferation was increased by IgG-PM/VT. Both IgG-PM and IgG-PM/VT decreased trophoblast invasion, which was restored by enoxaparin, aspirin, and ATL. IgG-PM triggered reduction in STAT3 phosphorylation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Some drugs used to prevent aPL-induced PM modulated the alteration of trophoblast function."
          }
        ]
      },
